From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 21:40:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] v4l2: hold fewer capture buffers for H.264 and HEVC streams

The decoder told the framework to keep 16 frames in flight for an H.264 or
HEVC stream, which the framework turns into 20 capture buffers. At 4K a
capture buffer is 11.87 MiB in the guest and again on the host, so a stream
held 237 MiB on each side. 16 is the deepest reordering a stream can have,
and suits a decoder that keeps the pictures it reorders in its capture
buffers. This device reorders on the host, and its capture buffers hold no
pictures that wait for their display order.

The framework stops queuing work once the works that wait for an output, with
their input released, reach the pipeline delay plus the output delay plus 4,
and the host releases a picture once more than the stream's reorder depth R of
them wait, so a stream stalls only when R exceeds the sum of the two delays
by more than 3. R is at most 16, so a sum of 13 stalls no stream. Only a work
that is done can tell the framework another delay, and a stream that needs the
whole limit completes none before it has it, so the sum is set for every
stream at the start rather than lowered for a stream that reorders little.

The framework sizes the capture buffer queue from the output delay and the
input buffer queue from the pipeline delay, each plus 4, and reads both once,
in one query, when it starts. A capture buffer costs 23.7 MiB at 4K (the
guest's and the host's picture), 5.9 MiB at 1080p, and an input block 4 and 2
MiB, so the sum of 13 is split by the picture size the client configures. A
picture above 1920x1088 takes 4 and 9: 8 capture buffers, not 14, and 13
input slots, not 7. At 4K that is 142 MiB less for the pictures and 24 MiB
more for the input blocks. A picture of that size or less takes 8 and 5: 12
capture buffers and 9 input slots. On an iPad guest of 2 vCPUs a 60 fps
1080p stream decoded 10 to 13 percent fewer frames with 8 capture buffers
than with 14 while the guest was busy (the first minutes after a boot, or a busy loop
beside the player), and 12 decoded as many as 14. At 1080p that holds 15.8
MiB more than 4 and 9 would and 7.9 MiB less than the old 10 and 3. The stall
limit stays the 17 works it was for every size.

Only the configured size decides. The picture size the device states for the
stream from its SPS changes neither the buffers the framework has sized nor
the limit, so a stream configured at 1080p whose SPS declares 4K keeps its 12
capture buffers, and one configured at 4K whose SPS declares 1080p keeps its
8; neither can stall. The V4L2 capture queue is sized from the delay the
instance reports.

The delays are a pure function of the size in DecodeDelays.h, which the
tests of the outer repository compile and run.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
